      "output": "Based on your Apple ecosystem and a budget under\u202f\u00a3700 (you tend to keep phones for 4+\u202fyears), the key criteria are:\n\n1. **iOS continuity** \u2013 seamless hand\u2011off with iPhone, Watch, iCloud, etc.  \n2. **Longevity of software support** \u2013 at least 5\u202fyears of iOS updates.  \n3. **Camera & battery that handle daily use** \u2013 good low\u2011light photos and a full day between charges.  \n4. **Value for money** \u2013 strong performance without paying for flagship\u2011only features you won\u2019t need.\n\n**Pick:** **iPhone SE (3rd\u202fgen, 2022)** \u2013 typically \u00a3429\u2013\u00a3479 for the 128\u202fGB model. It runs the A15 Bionic (same as iPhone\u202f13), gets iOS updates for many years, and fits comfortably in your hand. Battery life is solid for a compact phone, and the single\u2011lens camera is very capable for everyday snaps. It stays within your budget while giving you the full Apple ecosystem experience.\n\n**Runner\u2011up:** **iPhone\u202f13 (standard, 128\u202fGB)** \u2013 typically \u00a3599\u2013\u00a3649 refurbished or on sale. It offers a larger OLED display, dual\u2011camera system with better low\u2011light performance, and slightly longer battery life. It\u2019s still under \u00a3700 and will receive updates for a comparable period, but costs more for the extra screen size and camera flexibility you may not need.\n\n**Trade\u2011off:** The SE gives you core iPhone performance at a lower price but with a smaller display and single camera; the iPhone\u202f13 adds a bigger screen and better photography at a modest premium.\n\n**Next step:** Check current pricing (new, refurbished, or carrier deals) for the 128\u202fGB SE and iPhone\u202f13, then decide if the larger display of the 13 is worth the extra cost for your daily use. Let me know which features matter most to you (e.g., screen size, camera) so I can fine\u2011tune the recommendation.",
