      },
      "output": "**Assumption:** You\u2019re saving for a flat deposit in Dublin over the next\u202f3\u202fyears and want a concrete monthly target.\n\n**Mechanics & rule of thumb**  \n1. **Deposit size:** In Ireland a typical first\u2011time buyer aims for\u202f10\u201120\u202f% of the purchase price.  \n2. **Property price estimate:** Look up current average prices for the type/area you\u2019re interested in (e.g., a 2\u2011bed flat in Dublin city centre \u2248\u202f\u20ac350\u202fk, a suburban 2\u2011bed \u2248\u202f\u20ac300\u202fk).  \n3. **Target deposit:** Multiply the price by 0.10\u20110.20. For a \u20ac300\u202fk flat, a 15\u202f% deposit =\u202f\u20ac45\u202fk; for \u20ac350\u202fk, 15\u202f% =\u202f\u20ac52.5\u202fk.  \n4. **Monthly saving needed:** Divide the deposit by 36\u202fmonths. \u20ac45\u202fk \u2192\u202f\u2248\u202f\u20ac1,250\u202f/\u202fmonth; \u20ac52.5\u202fk \u2192\u202f\u2248\u202f\u20ac1,460\u202f/\u202fmonth.\n\n**Downside test**  \nIf a month\u2019s net cash flow drops by \u20ac300 (e.g., unexpected bill), the plan still works if you have a 1\u2011month emergency buffer (\u2248\u202f\u20ac1,500\u20112,000) to cover the shortfall without breaking the saving streak. Without that buffer, you\u2019d need to pause saving that month, extending the timeline.\n\n**Recommendation**  \nSet a **monthly savings target of \u20ac1,300\u2013\u20ac1,500**, adjusting the exact figure to the deposit percentage you\u2019re comfortable with (10\u202f% = lower target, 20\u202f% = higher target). Build a separate emergency fund of 1\u2011month expenses before committing fully.\n\n**Next actions (this week)**  \n1. Research current average prices for the flat type/area you want and pick a realistic purchase price.  \n2. Calculate 10\u202f% and 20\u202f% of that price; choose the deposit percentage that fits your comfort level.  \n3. Open a dedicated high\u2011interest savings account, transfer the first month\u2019s target amount, and set up an automatic monthly transfer.\n\n*This is general financial education, not personalized investment advice. For detailed budgeting or tax implications, consider consulting a licensed financial adviser.*",
