      "output": "**Assumption:** You\u2019re a mechanical\u2011engineering student preparing for a thermodynamics final on the 20th and can devote\u202f\u2248\u202f3\u202fhours each weekday and\u202f\u2248\u202f2\u202fhours each weekend day to focused study.\n\n| Day | Focus & Activities (\u22483\u202fh weekday / 2\u202fh weekend) |\n|-----|------------------------------------------------|\n| 1 | Review lecture notes\u202f\u00a7\u202f1\u20132 (basic concepts, laws). Summarize each law in one sentence. Do the end\u2011of\u2011chapter \u201cconcept\u2011check\u201d problems for \u00a7\u202f1. |\n| 2 | Work through worked examples on the First Law for closed systems. Re\u2011derive the energy balance from first principles; then solve 2 practice problems without looking at solutions. |\n| 3 | Study entropy: definition, reversible vs\u202firreversible processes. Create a 2\u2011column table (process | \u0394S) for the examples in the textbook. Attempt 3 entropy\u2011change problems. |\n| 4 | Focus on ideal\u2011gas relations and property tables. Memorize the key equations (PV=nRT, Cp\u2011Cv=R). Complete a set of 5 quick\u2011fire calculations (pressure, volume, temperature). |\n| 5 | Review cycles (Otto, Brayton, Rankine). Sketch each cycle on a PV diagram and label the four (or five) key points. Solve a single cycle efficiency problem for each. |\n| 6 | **Weekend:** Consolidate the week: redo any problems you got wrong, write a one\u2011page \u201ccheat sheet\u201d of formulas and sign conventions. |\n| 7 | **Weekend:** Practice a mixed\u2011topic mini\u2011test (10 problems covering laws, entropy, cycles). Time yourself (30\u202fmin). Review answers and note lingering gaps. |\n| 8 | Deep dive into open systems and control volumes. Derive the steady\u2011flow energy equation; solve 2 problems on turbines and compressors. |\n| 9 | Heat transfer basics (conduction, convection, radiation) as they appear in thermodynamics. Solve 3 problems involving overall heat\u2011transfer coefficients. |\n| 10 | Exergy analysis: definition and calculation. Work through a textbook example, then create your own simple exergy problem and solve it. |\n| 11 | Review all derivations (First Law, entropy, exergy). Write each derivation from memory on a blank sheet; compare with notes and correct any steps. |\n| 12 | Full\u2011length practice exam (timed, 2\u202fh). Use a past paper or instructor\u2011provided mock. Mark questions you miss. |\n| 13 | Targeted review: revisit every missed question, re\u2011solve with guidance notes. Reinforce weak areas with 2\u20113 additional problems each. |\n| 14 | Final polish: run through your cheat sheet, recite key formulas aloud, and do a quick 15\u2011minute \u201cflash\u2011card\u201d recall of concepts. Get a good night\u2019s sleep before the exam. |\n\n**Check:** Which thermodynamic concept (law, entropy, cycle, etc.) still feels least clear after Day\u202f7, and what specific type of problem would help you solidify it?",
